<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paul de Chomedey de Maisonneuve</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1612–1676</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Paul de Chomedey de Maisonneuve was a French officer who founded Ville-Marie, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a>, in May 1642 for the Société Notre-Dame de Montréal, a religious association that aimed to convert the Native peoples. He governed the settlement for more than twenty years, defending it against Iroquois attacks, until he was recalled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1665.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paul-de-chomedey-de-maisonneuve.jpg" alt="Paul de Chomedey de Maisonneuve" width="320" height="456" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Albert Ferland (1872-1943)D'après d'autres versions antérieures de la fin du XIXe siècle par d'autres illustrateurs [1]. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paul_Chomedey_de_Maisonneuve.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
